51: #  R6 shipped without a pass-2 review of its diff.  That was caught externally,
52: #  recorded, and corrected -- and R7 then did exactly the same thing one round
53: #  later.  So a mechanical check was written.  A pass-2 seat then MEASURED that
54: #  the check itself was vacuous: it sat below the "nothing staged" early exit, so
55: #  on a clean post-commit tree -- the only state in which a missed pass 2 is
56: #  actually detectable -- the script returned before reaching it.  It also keyed
57: #  on source mtime, which any checkout or copy moves, and accepted any panel
58: #  directory as evidence without asking what that panel reviewed.
59: #
60: #  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check, because it reassures.  This
61: #  version fixes all four faults:
62: #    * it runs BEFORE the early exit, so a clean tree still gets checked;
63: #    * it asks about the PREVIOUS src-touching COMMIT, not the working tree --
64: #      you cannot be required to have reviewed the commit you are about to make,
65: #      but you can be required to have reviewed the last one;
66: #    * the receipt is keyed to a SHA in a TRACKED file, which is attributable and
67: #      survives a different machine, where an mtime is neither;
68: #    * it is HARD, because both misses were invisible rather than argued.
69: #  It still cannot judge whether a review was any good.  Nothing mechanical can.
70: #  It guarantees one was RECORDED against the commit it reviewed, which is the
71: #  enforceable half.
72: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
73: #  G. EVERY SEAT THAT ANSWERED IN A CITED PANEL MUST BE RECORDED.
74: #
75: #  The dashboard renders three different things as the same blank cell: a seat
