2494: property and still passes?"** One extra invocation per round, higher yield than any ratio change.
2495: 
2496: **FIVE MECHANICAL RULES, all from real 2026-08-13 errors, all near-zero cost.** These would
2497: have prevented roughly six mistakes outright:
2498: 1. Write commit messages **from the diff**, never from intent (a message claimed a citation
2499:    fix that no hunk made).
2500: 2. When correcting a claim, **grep for its siblings** before calling it fixed (an "un-fakeable"
2501:    claim was corrected in one place and survived in three).
2502: 3. **Regenerate before grepping a generated file** � build-tree residue is not evidence about
2503:    the generator (four rounds of #88 read a stale `src/Makefile` as configure's output).
2504: 4. Every reproduction must answer **"did it fail for the reason under test?"** � a guard that
2505:    says "FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON, do not claim it" stopped two false results.
2506: 5. **Read the line before citing it** (one echo got cited four different ways across three
2507:    passes, each believing it was fixing the last).
2508: 
2509: **The goal is not "no mistakes" � that is unreachable and chasing it produced the six-pass
2510: chain. The goal is NO MISTAKE SURVIVES UNCHALLENGED**, which is achievable and which the
2511: panel already delivers: every error made on 2026-08-13 was caught, usually within one pass.
3374: > #### *** AND THE DERIVER MUST KEY ON WHAT DEFINES THE CLASS, NOT ON A CORRELATE OF IT.
3375: > #### Measured 2026-08-31 after THREE name-based censuses of one class all came back wrong,
3376: > #### in both directions -- and the third was mine, using the deriver I had just written
3377: > #### telling other people not to transcribe numbers. ***
3378: >
3379: > `staticlatchscope` counts function-static warn-once latches. Every census pattern keyed on
3380: > the VARIABLE BEING CALLED `w`/`warned`/`first`, and the population's names run **`warned` 13,
3381: > `w` 6, `first` 2, plus six singletons** (`warned2`, `warn_fs`, `warn`, `w_warned`,
3382: > `scc_oob_warned`, `r_warned`). Counts came back 10/3, then 23/7, then 26/10.
3383: >
3384: > **A latch is defined by its SCOPE and its USE, not by its spelling**, so a name pattern cannot
3385: > enumerate that class at all -- and every attempt undercounted while looking authoritative.
3386: > Keyed on scope (`grep -rc -e '^[[:space:]]\+static int ' -e '{[[:space:]]*static int '`) it is
3387: > **30 sites across 13 files, of which 28 across 11 are latches** -- the two exclusions being
3388: > periodic counters that the name patterns had also silently swept in.
3389: >
3390: > > **BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A MEMBER A MEMBER.** If the answer is a property
3391: > > the regex does not test -- scope, position, use, type -- the deriver is measuring a
3392: > > correlate, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible
3393: > > number. **This is the check-that-cannot-fail family's counting cousin: it returns the same
3394: > > shape of answer whether or not it is enumerating the right set.**
3395: >
